div.p:
The harbor though also seems to transcend the Lower East Side.  As a parting note, I would observe that at least one huge gap in the present study is the consideration of the New York harbor’s usefulness across traditions.  Within Jewish American literature, the harbor’s symbolic creation of citizenship seems like a particularly interesting site where the Jewish American origin narrative comes into contact with other groups of European immigrants.  Antin, Roth and Levinson, in their depiction of the New York harbor all tend to downplay the importance of Jewish religion in favor of presenting the Jew as sharing space with other immigrant groups sharing a common destiny as newly minted American citizens.  I have to wonder how this might complicate our understanding of Jewish American identity?  How are twentieth century Jews relating to an expansion of American citizenship beyond Jewish or even Eastern European identity?  What is the religious presence in the border crossing if it is an experience shared by others (Religion certainly seems implied in Antin’s

 Promised Land

)?  And finally, how does the successful appropriation of symbols such as the Statue of Liberty by Jews and others effect cultures excluded from cultural if not legal citizenship in the twentieth century?  Freighted as they are, these must wait for another time and another term.
